# CONSULT-54 (fresh Sonnet 5) — verdict: renderer/config mismatch (Thread A via config), not camera code, not game data

## Bottom line

The best-supported explanation is a **config-driven renderer mismatch**, not a render-side portal bug
in the old sense, and not different game data. Concretely: **our currently-staged Release cfg forces
`rasterMajor=11` (D3D11/gl11), while the actual reference binary the user played is physically incapable
of running D3D11 at all** (it ships no gl11 DLL). If the "OUR client see-through" repro was run via
`SwgClient_r.exe` (very likely — it's the freshly-rebuilt exe, `stage/SwgClient_r.exe` timestamped
2026-07-01 08:04, same day), the user was comparing **D3D9 (reference) against D3D11 (ours)**, not
same-renderer behavior. That reframes the whole question: this isn't necessarily "our camera goes
through the wall" at all — it may be a still-undiagnosed **gl11-specific portal/depth render bug**,
cosmetically resembling the old (already-fixed, D3D9/32-bit-only) cull-flip but mechanically unrelated.

## Evidence

### 1. Found the actual reference binary — and it can't run D3D11
`D:\Code\client-tools-koogie\stage\` is a fully staged, playable build from the client-tools lineage
(CMake-generated cfg, `SwgClient_d.exe` dated 2026-05-09). Its DLL set:
```
gl05_d.dll gl05_o.dll gl05_r.dll
gl06_d.dll gl06_o.dll gl06_r.dll
gl07_d.dll gl07_o.dll gl07_r.dll
```
**No gl11 DLL at all.** Its `client.cfg` has no `rasterMajor` key
(`D:\Code\client-tools-koogie\stage\client.cfg`), so it falls through to the engine default —
`src\engine\client\library\clientGraphics\src\win32\SetupClientGraphics.cpp:255/303` (`data.rasterMajor = 5`)
— i.e. **gl05 (D3D9)**, and that's the *only* family it could load even if configured otherwise.

### 2. Our Release cfg is currently forced to D3D11 with a self-contradicting comment
`D:\Code\swg-client-v2\stage\client.cfg:77`:
```
rasterMajor=11   # gl05/D3D9 32-bit: D3DCREATE_FPU_PRESERVE fix verify at the cantina portal spot
```
The **value is 11 (D3D11/gl11)** but the **comment describes a gl05/D3D9 verify** — this is leftover
state from an earlier RenderDoc diagnostic session (RenderDoc can't capture D3D9, so gl11 gets forced
for captures — see AGENTS.md) that was never reverted. `stage\client_d.cfg:41` (Debug) is `rasterMajor=5`.
So **Release and Debug currently point at two different renderer families on this machine.**
`stage/SwgClient_r.exe` was rebuilt today (2026-07-01 08:04) — i.e. whatever repro used the Release exe
most recently ran gl11.

### 3. The old "portal cull flip" fix is explicitly proven NOT to apply here
`src\engine\client\application\Direct3d9\src\win32\Direct3d9.cpp:1355-1361` (our tree, comment on the
`D3DCREATE_FPU_PRESERVE` fix):
> "x64 (SSE, no x87 mode) and D3D11 (never touches the FPU) are immune — which is exactly why the
> see-through reproduced ONLY on gl05/32-bit."

This is a **self-falsifying banner for the naive "same old bug resurfaced" theory** on two fronts:
x64 is the active milestone platform, AND D3D11 is explicitly immune by construction. If today's
symptom is happening on x64 and/or gl11, it is **not** a recurrence of the documented/fixed mechanism —
it's either (a) a new, undiagnosed gl11 render bug, or (b) genuine camera-position penetration with an
unrelated cause. Either way, "port more collision code" (banned framing) was never going to fix it,
and neither is re-applying the FPU_PRESERVE lesson — that lesson doesn't transfer to D3D11.

### 4. Portal/cell visibility is engine-side, not renderer-plugin code — but that cuts both ways
`grep -rn "portal" src/engine/client/library/clientGraphics/src/win32/Direct3d9/` and the `Direct3d11/`
equivalent both return **zero hits** in both trees. Portal/cell traversal lives in
`sharedObject/src/shared/portal/{CellProperty,PortalProperty}.cpp` (renderer-agnostic). That means a
renderer swap can't change *which cells are logically visible* — but it absolutely can change **how a
visible cell's contents are drawn relative to depth/near-plane**, which is a distinct failure mode from
cull logic: e.g. a wrong/missing depth-clear, a stale bound depth-stencil view, or a per-cell draw-order
issue on the gl11 path could make exterior geometry paint over an interior wall **that IS correctly
culled/visible** — indistinguishable from "see-through" to the eye, zero relation to camera collision.
This project's own memory has a long tail of exactly these gl11-only symptoms with no D3D9 analog:
additive-UI premultiply cyan fill, black bump walls from a cache-skip-zero-tail bug, blue face tint from
a bake tombstone, an IB-cache use-after-free that corrupted rendering ~14s into a scene, and terrain
blend-factor loss. A gl11-only depth/draw-order bug at building interior walls would fit this pattern
precisely and would require **zero** change to camera or collision code — consistent with the given fact
that FreeChaseCamera/ClientWorld::collide/CollisionWorld/CollideParameters are byte-identical.

### 5. Game data is NOT a plausible differentiator — ruled out
Thread B asked whether the reference ships different `camera/freechasecamera.iff` data or points at
different TRE data. It doesn't need to: `client-tools-koogie\stage\client.cfg` points
`TOCTreePath`/`searchTOC_*` at **the exact same `D:/Code/SWGSource Client v3.0/`** install we use. Same
TRE archives → same `camera/freechasecamera.iff` bytes, same building/POB collision-mesh data, for both
"OURS" and "the reference binary the user actually played." This kills the "different install/different
collision data" hypothesis outright — it is provably the same data.

### 6. Config defaults that DO transfer are identical, not a factor
- `cameraSimpleCollision` (`ms_cameraSimpleCollision`, `FreeChaseCamera.cpp:81`/`166`): a
  `LocalMachineOptionManager` option, zero-initialized `false` in both trees identically (declaration is
  identical in both trees) → both use the 4-ray diamond path unless a user profile overrides it. Neither
  `stage*/client*.cfg` nor `client-tools-koogie/stage/client.cfg` set it (it's a *LocalMachineOption*, not
  a `.cfg` key at all, actually — registered per-user via the options file/registry, not `ConfigFile`).
- `freeChaseCameraMaximumZoom` (`KEY_FLOAT(freeChaseCameraMaximumZoom, 1.f)`) and `cameraNearPlane`
  (`KEY_FLOAT(cameraNearPlane, 0.25f)`) in `ConfigClientGame.cpp` are byte-identical defaults in both
  trees, and neither is overridden in any of the three `.cfg` files inspected.
- `FloatingPointModel` (`/fp:fast` vs `/fp:precise`) is **not set explicitly** in either tree's `.vcxproj`
  files (checked `src/build/win32` in both) — both default to MSVC's `/fp:precise`. So there's no
  compiler-FP-mode analog of the old device-FPU-mode bug either.

## Recommended next diagnostic (concrete, ordered)

1. **Pin down which exe/cfg produced the "OUR client see-through" repro.** Check `rasterMajor` in
   whichever of `stage/client.cfg` (Release) / `stage/client_d.cfg` (Debug) was actually loaded. Given
   `stage/client.cfg:77` currently reads `rasterMajor=11` and `SwgClient_r.exe` was rebuilt today, the
   default assumption should be **gl11 was active**, not gl05.
2. **Re-run the exact repro forcing `rasterMajor=5`** (edit `stage/client.cfg` back to 5 — BOM-safe via
   Edit/Write, not PowerShell `Set-Content`) against `SwgClient_r.exe`. If the see-through **disappears**
   on gl05, this is conclusively a gl11 render bug — drop the camera-collision framing entirely, drop the
   interior-zoom-cap band-aid investigation, and open a RenderDoc D3D11 capture of the repro instead
   (`renderdoc-cli.exe … pixel_history` at the wall pixel, and `debug pixel --trace` to see what's
   actually driving the color — same tool used to root-cause the additive-premultiply cyan bug).
3. **If it still reproduces on gl05** (ruling out gl11-only render bug), the remaining candidates are (a)
   genuine camera-endpoint penetration from a subtle geometric edge case in the *shared* algorithm (the
   brief allows this) or (b) an interior-geometry portal/collision-mesh boundary condition specific to
   the building being tested. Next step there: log `ClientWorld::collide(...)`'s returned `result.getPoint()`
   each frame next to the wall polygon plane distance, to directly distinguish "camera position crossed
   the plane" (real penetration) from "camera stayed on the room side but the far side rendered anyway"
   (still a render artifact, just not gl11-specific).
4. Either way, **stop treating the 2026-06-20 `D3DCREATE_FPU_PRESERVE` fix and its cantina writeup as
   evidence for the current bug** — its own comment proves it's inert on both D3D11 and x64/SSE, which
   covers essentially every config this investigation would run under going forward (x64 is the active
   platform per AGENTS.md).
